### What is distributed tracing?

A trace follows a single request across multiple services. It is identified by a trace ID shared across all services. Each unit of work is a span with its own span ID and a parent span ID, forming a tree. The root span starts in the first service, and each downstream call creates a child span.

```text
Trace ID: abc-123
├── transfer-service (root span, 150ms)
│   ├── account-service (child span, 80ms)
│   │   └── ledger-db (child span, 30ms)
│   └── notification-service (child span, 20ms)
```

### How does trace context travel between services?

In gRPC, the carrier is call metadata (gRPC's equivalent of HTTP headers). The client injects the active context into metadata before the call leaves, and the server extracts it from incoming metadata. The standard format is W3C `traceparent`, carried in a metadata key.

```java
package com.bank.tracing;

import io.grpc.*;
import io.opentelemetry.api.trace.Span;
import io.opentelemetry.api.trace.Tracer;
import io.opentelemetry.context.Context;
import io.opentelemetry.context.propagation.TextMapSetter;

public class TracingClientInterceptor implements ClientInterceptor {

    private static final TextMapSetter<Metadata> SETTER =
        (carrier, key, value) -> carrier.put(
            Metadata.Key.of(key, Metadata.ASCII_STRING_MARSHALLER), value);

    @Override
    public <Req, Resp> ClientCall<Req, Resp> interceptCall(
            MethodDescriptor<Req, Resp> method,
            CallOptions options,
            Channel next) {

        return new ForwardingClientCall.SimpleForwardingClientCall<>(
                next.newCall(method, options)) {
            @Override
            public void start(Listener<Resp> listener, Metadata headers) {
                // Inject trace context into outgoing metadata
                io.opentelemetry.api.GlobalOpenTelemetry.getPropagators()
                    .getTextMapPropagator()
                    .inject(Context.current(), headers, SETTER);
                super.start(listener, headers);
            }
        };
    }
}
```

### How does the server interceptor extract context?

The server interceptor reads trace context from incoming metadata and starts a child span. The child span becomes the current span for the duration of the handler.

```java
package com.bank.tracing;

import io.grpc.*;
import io.opentelemetry.api.GlobalOpenTelemetry;
import io.opentelemetry.api.trace.Span;
import io.opentelemetry.api.trace.Tracer;
import io.opentelemetry.context.Context;
import io.opentelemetry.context.Scope;
import io.opentelemetry.context.propagation.TextMapGetter;

public class TracingServerInterceptor implements ServerInterceptor {

    private static final TextMapGetter<Metadata> GETTER =
        new TextMapGetter<>() {
            @Override
            public Iterable<String> keys(Metadata carrier) {
                return carrier.keys();
            }
            @Override
            public String get(Metadata carrier, String key) {
                var k = Metadata.Key.of(key, Metadata.ASCII_STRING_MARSHALLER);
                return carrier.get(k);
            }
        };

    private final Tracer tracer = GlobalOpenTelemetry.getTracer("bank-service");

    @Override
    public <Req, Resp> ServerCall.Listener<Req> interceptCall(
            ServerCall<Req, Resp> call,
            Metadata headers,
            ServerCallHandler<Req, Resp> next) {

        // Extract parent context from incoming metadata
        Context parentCtx = GlobalOpenTelemetry.getPropagators()
            .getTextMapPropagator()
            .extract(Context.current(), headers, GETTER);

        // Start a child span
        Span span = tracer.spanBuilder(call.getMethodDescriptor().getFullMethodName())
            .setParent(parentCtx)
            .startSpan();

        try (Scope scope = span.makeCurrent()) {
            return next.startCall(call, headers);
        } finally {
            span.end();
        }
    }
}
```

### How do you use OpenTelemetry's ready-made instrumentation?

In practice you rarely hand-write interceptors. OpenTelemetry ships ready-made gRPC instrumentation that registers both interceptors automatically.

```xml
<!-- Maven dependency -->
<dependency>
    <groupId>io.opentelemetry.instrumentation</groupId>
    <artifactId>opentelemetry-grpc-1.6</artifactId>
    <version>2.0.0</version>
</dependency>
```

```bash
# Run with the OpenTelemetry Java agent (auto-instruments gRPC)
java -javaagent:opentelemetry-javaagent.jar \
     -Dotel.exporter.otlp.endpoint=http://collector:4317 \
     -Dotel.service.name=transfer-service \
     -jar transfer-service.jar
```

### How do you configure the exporter and sampler?

The exporter sends spans to a backend. The sampler controls how many traces are recorded to balance cost and coverage.

```java
package com.bank.config;

import io.opentelemetry.sdk.OpenTelemetrySdk;
import io.opentelemetry.sdk.trace.SdkTracerProvider;
import io.opentelemetry.sdk.trace.export.BatchSpanProcessor;
import io.opentelemetry.exporter.otlp.trace.OtlpGrpcSpanExporter;
import io.opentelemetry.sdk.trace.samplers.Sampler;

public class TracingConfig {
    public static OpenTelemetrySdk configure() {
        OtlpGrpcSpanExporter exporter = OtlpGrpcSpanExporter.builder()
            .setEndpoint("http://collector:4317")
            .build();

        SdkTracerProvider tracerProvider = SdkTracerProvider.builder()
            .addSpanProcessor(BatchSpanProcessor.builder(exporter).build())
            .setSampler(Sampler.traceIdRatioBased(0.1)) // 10% sampling
            .build();

        return OpenTelemetrySdk.builder()
            .setTracerProvider(tracerProvider)
            .build();
    }
}
```

### How do you correlate traces with logs?

Inject the trace ID into the logging MDC so every log line links to its trace. This lets you search logs by trace ID in your logging platform.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import io.opentelemetry.api.trace.Span;
import org.slf4j.Logger;
import org.slf4j.LoggerFactory;
import org.slf4j.MDC;

public class TransferService {
    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(TransferService.class);

    public void transfer(String from, String to, String amount) {
        Span span = Span.current();
        MDC.put("traceId", span.getSpanContext().getTraceId());
        MDC.put("spanId", span.getSpanContext().getSpanId());

        log.info("Transfer {} -> {} amount {}", from, to, amount);
        // Business logic...

        MDC.clear();
    }
}
```

### What are the security considerations for banking?

Never put sensitive PII into span attributes. Account numbers, balances, and customer names should not appear in traces stored in a backend like Jaeger.

```java
// WRONG: PII in span attributes
span.setAttribute("account.number", "RAJESH-12345678");
span.setAttribute("balance", "50000.00");

// CORRECT: use safe identifiers
span.setAttribute("account.type", "savings");
span.setAttribute("transfer.amount.bucket", "10K-50K");
```

### What about async boundaries and thread pools?

The active span context is thread-local. When work hops threads, you must capture and restore the context. OpenTelemetry provides `Context.wrap()` for this.

```java
import io.opentelemetry.context.Context;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

// Wrap the task to propagate context across threads
ExecutorService pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(4);
Context current = Context.current();
pool.submit(current.wrap(() -> {
    // Trace context is available here
    processTransfer();
}));
```

